        "content": "{\n  \"status\": \"unmet\",\n  \"material_usefulness\": \"supporting\",\n  \"useful_material\": \"This review identifies inosine and short-chain fatty acids (SCFAs) as key microbial metabolites influencing immune checkpoint blockade (ICB) efficacy. It reports that elevated fecal levels of acetate, propionate, butyrate, and valerate are associated with prolonged progression-free survival (PFS) and better treatment response in a prospective study of 52 solid tumor patients receiving anti-PD-1 therapy. It also cites evidence that high systemic butyrate and propionate levels correlate with CTLA-4 blockade resistance. Regarding inosine, the review describes a mechanism where B. pseudolongum-produced inosine translocates systemically to activate antitumor T cells and improve ICI efficacy. The text provides specific citation markers (e.g., [112], [156], [161]) pointing to primary studies that likely contain the raw concentration data and statistical associations required.\",\n  \"auxiliary_material\": \"The review provides a specific concentration threshold for a different metabolite, noting that low serum L-arginine concentration (less than 42 μM) is associated with poor outcomes in anti-PD-1 therapy, demonstrating the type of data available for metabolites in this field. It also outlines general mechanisms such as SCFA-mediated HDAC inhibition and GPR signaling enhancing CD8+ T cell function, and inosine-mediated adenosine receptor activation. The text lists multiple clinical trials (Table 1, Table 2) involving microbiota interventions but does not detail metabolite concentration outcomes within those trial descriptions.\",\n  \"remaining_gap\": \"The supplied text does not report the specific absolute concentration values (μM or ng/mL) for inosine or SCFAs in human tumor microenvironment (TME) or paired serum. While it mentions 'systemic' and 'fecal' levels and their correlation with PFS or response, the quantitative data points are not reproduced in this review. Additionally, the distinction between TME concentrations versus serum concentrations is not explicitly quantified for the target metabolites in the provided text.\",\n  \"outline_action\": \"Retrieve the full texts of the primary studies cited in this review, specifically Mager et al. (referenced as [112] for inosine), the prospective study of 52 patients (referenced as [156] or [111] for SCFAs), and Coutzac et al. (referenced as [161] for systemic butyrate/propionate). Extract the specific serum or TME concentration ranges and the statistical metrics (e.g., hazard ratios, p-values for ORR/PFS) from those original sources to fulfill the success criteria.\"\n}",
